│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃           Patch for autodoc.inherited-members issue            ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue arises because the autodoc module is not including the   │
│ baseclass namespace in the search key when looking for inherited   │
│ attributes. This causes the inherited-members directive to fail to │
│ capture attributes from base classes.                              │
│                                                                    │
│ The fix involves modifying the logic that constructs the search    │
│ key to include the baseclass namespace. This ensures that          │
│ attributes from base classes are properly recognized during        │
│ documentation.                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│ [file]sphinx/ext/autodoc.py[/file] [original] def                  │
│ autodoc_inherited_members(self, member): # Original logic that     │
│ fails to include baseclass namespace search_key = (member.name,    │
│ member.objname) return search_key [/original] [patched] def        │
│ autodoc_inherited_members(self, member): # Modified logic to       │
│ include baseclass namespace base_class = getattr(member,           │
│ 'base_class', None) if base_class: search_key = (member.name,      │
│ member.objname, base_class.name) else: search_key = (member.name,  │
│ member.objname) return search_key [/patched] [/file]               │
╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
